00:01:08.412 --> 00:01:10.370
I've been in this space
for a really long time.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:13.990
I've been both a developer and
a head of duct tape, a.k.a.

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.012
head of operations,
and a project manager,

00:01:16.012 --> 00:01:17.220
and all these kind of things.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.560
So I've seen lots of pain from
lots of different angles here.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.930
One of the things
I did a lot of when

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:25.480
I was a head of strategy
in this space was I

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:26.740
showed a slide like this.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.230
Every enterprise must have seen
this slide 50 million times.

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:31.930
It's the classic
slide that says,

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.040
if you don't find
a way to speed up,

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.450
you are going to go bankrupt.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.540
And there's lots of good
data to back this up.

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.740
And so it's a really
genuine problem.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.180
And thus, I think
it's legitimate

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.280
that we all think about it.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:47.080
But by and large,
people are convinced

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.630
that this is genuinely
an issue to look at.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:51.700
And what then tends to
happen, and this is, I

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.090
think, where an important
lesson comes in,

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:57.450
is that you start drawing
a diagram like this, right?

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.690
Oh, my software
delivery pipeline.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.435
Source code over here.

00:02:02.435 --> 00:02:05.330
Got just a few
things I need to do,

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.730
and then I'm going
to be in production.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:09.830
It kind of looks sort of
simple and compelling.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:11.205
And you could
imagine if you have

00:02:11.205 --> 00:02:15.860
a simple website with some code
that that could sort of work.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:17.600
But of course, the
reality, first of all,

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.360
is that it's actually more
of a tooling landscape.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.470
It looks a little bit like this.

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:24.620
And so it's an enormous
integration game

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.050
that you already
start to have to play.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.390
And if you try to
play that integration

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:33.150
game all across the
pipeline in one go,

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:36.050
it's very quickly a boil
the ocean type problem.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.990
And I think we all know
how this goes wrong.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:44.060
And there's just too much
to tackle to get this right.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.280
So I think an important
lesson to start out

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:49.880
with is that not just
there's a big challenge lying

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:52.505
under this deceptively
simple diagram.

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:54.630
But I think there's another
really important point.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.370
And that is conceptually
thinking of it as this,

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.090
if I just go back one--

00:02:59.090 --> 00:02:59.810
does this work?

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:00.650
Can I go back?

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:03.340
Thinking of it as this
conceptually simple left

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:07.750
to right, just do all the
things diagram obscures the fact

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:11.380
that there's actually two
related but different processes

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:12.680
at work here.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.640
And so the way I
draw this diagram now

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.590
is a little bit more like this.

00:03:17.590 --> 00:03:19.840
I don't really use
the term CI and CD

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.060
very much because
they kind of imply

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.880
automation and more automation.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.640
Instead, I talk about
the developer feedback

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:30.420
cycle, and the
application release

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.440
cycle on the other side.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:35.490
And I find that
framing helps me a lot

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.210
because, A, if you look
at what we do as Google

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:42.020
or what many other companies do,
your developer feedback cycle,

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:45.020
you want that to run every
single time you propose

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:46.700
or commit a code change.

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.950
And if any of you heard the
Cloud build announcement--

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:50.530
I'm sure a lot of you did--

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:51.980
Google's doing a
lot in this space

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.680
to provide great tooling to
make that really easy and really

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:55.550
efficient.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:57.440
And this is all about
speed and efficiency,

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.050
and getting great information
back to your developers

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:03.070
as quickly as possible.

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:06.090
But then on the other side, you
have the application release

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.910
cycle, which maybe you
want to do every time

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.820
you commit a line of
code, or maybe not.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.480
Maybe you do it weekly, or
every sprint, or whatever.

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.790
And here, there are
many different triggers.

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.040
And the goals are also
somewhat different.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.959
You're typically not
worried all that much

00:04:21.959 --> 00:04:24.670
about doing this as
fast as possible.

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:26.820
You're worried about doing
it in a compliant way,

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.570
in a way that prevents
risk from creating

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:33.490
problems for your organization,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:34.710
et cetera.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:40.114
Safe, compliance, all these kind
of adjectives come into play.

00:04:40.114 --> 00:04:41.530
And if I zoom in
a little bit more

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:45.130
specifically on that second half
of this pipeline, application

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:49.464
release, one of actually three
components from my perspective,

00:04:49.464 --> 00:04:51.130
or from our perspective,
that are really

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:53.230
important to think about here.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.260
Very often, if you think of it
as a pure automation problem,

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:58.090
you only really think
about the middle one,

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.790
application deployment,
which is those steps

00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:03.850
that you need to take to get
your bits and bytes actually

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:05.650
into production.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.690
But there's two other
very important parts

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:11.500
that we see a lot of people
recognizing at large scale.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:14.710
One is the simple notion
of, how do I even understand

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:16.320
what is running where?

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.100
That's not a process problem.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.540
That's a problem
of understanding

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:21.070
your current state.

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:23.770
And sure, if you have a
single runtime in a single GCP

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:25.700
project, that's not a big deal.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:27.670
But if you're doing
multi-cluster,

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:30.630
multi-cloud, maybe different
types of run times,

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.820
it starts to become
a huge problem.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.620
And not only do you want to
understand what's actually

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.390
out there, you
probably also want

00:05:37.390 --> 00:05:40.300
to act on it in
certain limited ways.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.410
You want to roll back.

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:42.610
You want to scale up.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:43.870
You want to scale down.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:47.390
You want to understand
health, and so on.

00:05:47.390 --> 00:05:49.910
And then another aspect
of the deployment process

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:52.220
specifically, or the
application release process,

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:56.510
is that there's a bunch of
domain-specific best practices

00:05:56.510 --> 00:06:00.019
that are really important
to implement in this area,

00:06:00.019 --> 00:06:02.560
and that ideally you would like
tooling in this space to give

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:03.727
you out-of-the-box.

00:06:03.727 --> 00:06:05.810
Whether it's things like
immutable infrastructure,

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:09.020
or good support for
rollbacks, or canary analysis,

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:12.110
or all these other kinds of
things, like everything else,

00:06:12.110 --> 00:06:13.436
they can all be scripted.

00:06:13.436 --> 00:06:14.810
But it's much,
much better if you

00:06:14.810 --> 00:06:17.280
can provide this
functionality out of the box.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.892
I'm not going to go
through all of them.

00:06:19.892 --> 00:06:21.850
And then, obviously, from
a perspective of what

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:24.520
we know enterprises want,
and indeed, most developers

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:28.330
and operations teams want, great
to have all this functionality,

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:30.080
but we want it to be secure.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:31.720
We ideally want it
to be open so it's

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:33.640
extensible and understandable.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.950
We would like hybrid
and multi-cloud support.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:38.530
And we want it to support
all the other 50,000

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:40.410
things you remember
from that slide I

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:41.710
showed a little earlier.

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:44.830
Because, of course, we can't
throw away everything we have.

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:47.540
We need to leverage our
existing investments, et cetera,

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:49.570
et cetera, et cetera.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:52.230
So this is me teeing up
Spinnaker here, effectively.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:55.170
This is an open-source
project which was originally

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.720
conceived-- well, the
predecessor version actually

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:00.600
was conceived as Netflix's
continuous delivery

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:03.220
platform for this part
of their life cycle.

00:07:03.220 --> 00:07:06.600
And it's now a very successful
open-source project used by--

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:09.780
John will talk about how
Target uses it, but used

00:07:09.780 --> 00:07:13.140
by Netflix, and Google, and
many, many other companies.

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:15.060
Obviously supports
all the runtimes

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.371
that we care about,
Kubernetes, big topic

00:07:17.371 --> 00:07:18.870
here, but all the
other ones that we

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:21.139
know across multiple clouds.

00:07:21.139 --> 00:07:22.680
And there's a lot
of people using it.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:24.360
I won't bore you
with a logo slide.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:27.984
It's a nice one
to look at later.

00:07:27.984 --> 00:07:30.400
Going back to the four key
attributes that I talked about,

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:32.240
one of them was open.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.327
And we find that very
important for our customers,

00:07:34.327 --> 00:07:36.160
not just because that
gives them flexibility

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.440
in where and how
they run it, but also

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:40.990
because it gives companies'
release and platform

00:07:40.990 --> 00:07:44.650
engineering teams the capability
to tailor it to their needs,

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:47.740
and to match the requirements
that every company comes up

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:50.410
against, and that is somewhat
different in their contexts.

00:07:50.410 --> 00:07:52.472
So with that, let me
hand it over to John,

00:07:52.472 --> 00:07:54.930
who will talk about exactly
how Target solved this problem.

00:08:01.404 --> 00:08:03.476
JOHN ENGELMAN: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:08:03.476 --> 00:08:05.540
I'm very excited to
be up here talking

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:09.360
about Target's journey
on this path to today.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:10.360
My name's John Engelman.

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:12.568
I'm a principal engineer
with the release and runtime

00:08:12.568 --> 00:08:13.810
engineering group at Target.

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:18.199
And I basically do what Andrew
does as well, but for Target.

00:08:18.199 --> 00:08:19.990
I'm also a regular
open-source contributor.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:22.480
I've been working in kind
of the build automation

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:27.210
and deployment in cloud spaces
for a number of years now.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:30.850
And prior to coming to Target,
I was working as a consultant

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:33.909
and doing this for everything
from 10-person companies

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:36.250
up to today where I'm
doing it for a Fortune 50

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:41.400
company in a number
of different spaces.

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:45.800
So before I get into how
Target does this today,

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:48.560
I want to give you a
little bit of idea,

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:50.960
and I want to reinforce
some of what Andrew

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:53.060
was saying just a little bit.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:56.840
So first of all, from my
experience as a consultant,

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.840
this journey is not
unique to Target.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:01.510
This is the same
path that I've seen

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:04.550
many, many companies go on.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:06.440
I'm also not going to
stand here and tell you

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:08.540
the answer to this
problem, right,

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:11.160
because there isn't
one to answer.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:12.860
I find that building
platforms, and

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.480
in particular this
application delivery space,

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.930
there's not a single solution
because the solutions have

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:20.600
to be geared for your
particular business

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:22.290
and technical
requirements, right?

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:25.400
It's almost a very personal
engineering problem

00:09:25.400 --> 00:09:29.280
space to have happen.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.890
Also, just simply, everything
Andrew said is right.

00:09:34.890 --> 00:09:37.320
When we started putting
this talk together,

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:41.640
it was very interesting how
quickly our ideas coalesced

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:44.010
around the same things.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:47.246
One of the issues that I
see in the industry right

00:09:47.246 --> 00:09:48.870
now is that we have
a tendency to refer

00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:53.340
to CI/CD as like a singular,
composite concept, right?

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:54.840
I have a CI/CD tool.

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:57.120
I'm doing CI/CD, et cetera.

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:00.750
But as Andrew was saying,
they are separate, right?

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:03.210
So continuous integration is
about modeling that software

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.070
development cycle,
how developers

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:06.490
are getting feedback.

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:09.690
How are we increasing
velocity and increasing

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:14.400
the quality of our code by
decreasing that cycle time?

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.100
And ultimately, the
outcome of that cycle

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:21.240
is that we're producing
a deployable artifact

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.509
that we consider good
based on the information

00:10:23.509 --> 00:10:24.300
that we have today.

00:10:26.725 --> 00:10:28.350
So one of the things
that we strive for

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:30.300
is to get this automated
code coverage that

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:32.100
covers everything.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.800
And if anyone says that
they're aiming and striving

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:38.430
for 100% test coverage, that's
great, and we should do that,

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:41.610
but it's absolutely not
required to be successful

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:44.460
at continuous
integration, right?

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.490
And on the other side,
continuous deployment

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:48.420
is about that software
delivery cycle, right?

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:52.470
And here, the real purpose
is to decrease the impact

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:55.500
to customers of disruptive
operational actions

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.460
that we're taking on
our application, right?

00:10:57.460 --> 00:11:00.900
And our outcome is
to make available

00:11:00.900 --> 00:11:02.970
a new version of
our software and be

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:06.510
able to recover from any
possible regressions that

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:11.070
happened during that deployment.

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:13.080
So in this sense,
both of these actions

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:15.510
are orchestrated workflows,
but with very different

00:11:15.510 --> 00:11:17.070
requirements, right?

00:11:17.070 --> 00:11:19.650
And if you look at
it, a CI workflow

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:22.170
is very linear in
general, where,

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:25.140
if an error occurs
along that line,

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:27.000
we basically say stop, right?

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:30.720
Just stop, fail out,
don't do anything more.

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:33.840
And they're generally
very short running,

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:35.790
whereas in continuous
delivery, we

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.590
have this very complex
logic of what to do

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:39.840
when we're rolling out, right?

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:43.200
There's external forces
acting that are telling us

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.250
what to do next.

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:47.520
And, in a failure case, how
do we recover from that?

00:11:47.520 --> 00:11:49.410
And these continuous
delivery workflows

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:52.050
can be very long-running,
anywhere from minutes

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.750
to hours to days or even
longer, depending on what type

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:58.590
of system we're rolling out.

00:11:58.590 --> 00:12:01.590
So to summarize that,
CI and CD, really,

00:12:01.590 --> 00:12:04.369
are two disparate ideas.

00:12:04.369 --> 00:12:05.910
And it doesn't imply
that we're using

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:08.126
a single tool to solve them.

00:12:08.126 --> 00:12:10.500
If you have a single tool that
solves both those problems

00:12:10.500 --> 00:12:11.700
and it works for
you, that's great.

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:12.720
But we don't have to.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:17.230
So, Target.

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:19.720
And how are we solving
some of these problems?

00:12:19.720 --> 00:12:21.600
So before I tell you
what we're doing today

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:23.350
and what we're going
to be doing tomorrow,

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:25.020
I've got to tell you
where we came from

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:27.100
and what we were
doing yesterday.

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:30.100
So let's rewind the
clock five years.

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:31.950
So long before I joined Target--

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:34.800
and we were a traditional
enterprise organization, right?

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.360
We had development teams.

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:38.040
We had operations teams.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:40.800
And developers built code,
and threw that over the wall

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:43.170
to operations teams that
deployed it and monitored

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:46.890
it and managed it, so a
very traditional workflow,

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:47.740
in that sense.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:50.790
Our delivery cycles were tied
to business and financial

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:54.230
calendars, and all that stuff.

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:56.650
And I think any time that you
look at a state of a system

00:12:56.650 --> 00:12:59.740
or a process or an
organization, there

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:01.720
are pros and cons to
both those things, right?

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:05.080
So one of the pros for this
kind of traditional enterprise,

00:13:05.080 --> 00:13:08.260
even though most of us would say
there's nothing pro about it,

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:10.930
is that we did have de-coupled
development and delivery

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:12.010
cycles.

00:13:12.010 --> 00:13:14.230
Now, it wasn't a
great decoupling

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:17.000
because we ended up in
these large, big bang

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:19.690
deliveries with intensive
integration cycles

00:13:19.690 --> 00:13:21.380
and things like that.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:23.710
And we also got into that
siloing of responsibilities,

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:25.200
right?

00:13:25.200 --> 00:13:28.630
And when everyone thinks
someone else is responsible,

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:30.890
ultimately, no one ends
up being responsible.

00:13:30.890 --> 00:13:32.820
And that's really coming
back to the TL;DR,

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:35.970
is just that we weren't
doing DevOps yet.

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:37.990
And this is a very
common starting point

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:43.360
for many, many enterprises
or large organizations.

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:45.090
So we knew we needed to change.

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:47.910
And our developers actually kind
of took that into their hand

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:51.870
and started to organically
grow the usage of Chef

00:13:51.870 --> 00:13:55.440
as a configuration automation
tool within Target.

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:58.170
This allowed them to reliably
provision new servers

00:13:58.170 --> 00:13:59.440
in a very consistent manner.

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:01.287
We were dropping our
times of how long it

00:14:01.287 --> 00:14:03.870
took to get a server out there
and running very, very quickly,

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:07.320
from potentially weeks to hours.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:12.704
So we started introducing
some commonality

00:14:12.704 --> 00:14:13.620
through our processes.

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:15.244
We were able to write
some higher order

00:14:15.244 --> 00:14:16.930
tests and functions.

00:14:16.930 --> 00:14:19.380
We started sharing knowledge
among our development teams

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:21.840
about, what are the best
practices for really doing

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:23.170
this?

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:27.120
But on the other side, we went
from this de-coupled cycle

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:31.170
into now a process
where we were conflating

00:14:31.170 --> 00:14:34.650
runtime configuration
with application versions

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:38.505
and application delivery.

00:14:38.505 --> 00:14:40.630
We're obviously still using
mutable infrastructure,

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:43.270
so things like rollback
were very difficult.

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:46.530
And because it grew organically
and naturally out of dev teams,

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:49.140
there was no real
guidance, right?

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:51.900
So we started seeing some
anti-patterns developing

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:56.060
within the tooling and
implementation that we had.

00:14:56.060 --> 00:14:58.040
So we knew we needed to
provide some structure

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:03.560
for our developers and users
to encode these best practices.

00:15:03.560 --> 00:15:06.070
And one of those is that we
shouldn't use a configuration

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:09.280
management tool as a general
purpose orchestrator, right?

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:12.700
If we're just trying to
automate fluid tasks,

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:17.860
there's probably better tools
situated for that purpose.

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:21.542
And finally, we knew
we needed to model

00:15:21.542 --> 00:15:23.250
the actual deployment
from the ground up,

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:27.940
and not just the runtime
of the application.

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:33.400
So after about a year of working
with Chef in the organization,

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:38.210
we started building a new
internal tool called Houston.

00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:40.650
And Houston was an
infrastructure management tool.

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:43.700
So it was built around our
native infrastructure provider

00:15:43.700 --> 00:15:44.660
APIs.

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:46.550
It used their tooling.

00:15:46.550 --> 00:15:48.950
It still used Chef
underneath the hood

00:15:48.950 --> 00:15:53.060
to provide runtime
configuration.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:58.340
And again, it gave us a step to
more closely model and automate

00:15:58.340 --> 00:16:01.300
more of what's needed to get
an application out and running.

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:04.630
And it further
reduced that time.

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:07.120
So we started introducing
concepts such as infrastructure

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:11.130
as code, deployment strategies
to our development teams.

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:13.500
We further reduced that time.

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:16.650
But on the other side, we
created a homegrown tool

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:19.380
that offered no support out
in the greater community.

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:21.810
When our devs had an
issue, they couldn't

00:16:21.810 --> 00:16:24.240
go to Stack Overflow or
Google and find the answer

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:25.590
to the problem.

00:16:25.590 --> 00:16:30.000
We couldn't source
any work or additions

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:33.230
to the tool from
outside of Target,

00:16:33.230 --> 00:16:37.890
and not even from inside Target
because the code was so custom

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:41.400
that just the team that built
it was able to support it.

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:43.860
And the biggest mistake
that we made in Houston

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:47.340
was that we introduced our own
language to what an application

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:48.390
deployment was.

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:54.316
It had this idea of missions
and orbits and things like that.

00:16:54.316 --> 00:16:55.880
And what we've
learned from that is

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:59.120
that we had built this tool
strictly from the vision

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:02.540
of, how are we going
to automate and make

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:06.560
infrastructure development
easier and better, right?

00:17:06.560 --> 00:17:09.530
And that doesn't always
work for developers, right?

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:11.900
So we had this impedance
mismatch, right?

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:15.500
We had built a tool that was
really, really good managing

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:18.030
automated infrastructure,
but not so good at modeling

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:19.010
an application itself.

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:23.260
And we also learned
that we just shouldn't

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:26.680
try to create models
and abstractions that

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:29.590
don't convey what it is, right?

00:17:29.590 --> 00:17:31.900
You have to talk about your
application deployments

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:35.290
in a way that is linked to the
practical concepts and features

00:17:35.290 --> 00:17:36.940
that they represent, right?

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:39.430
So we don't call a dog
an orange because it's

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:41.080
a dog, not an orange, right?

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:43.090
So we should call
a server a server,

00:17:43.090 --> 00:17:44.680
or an application
an application.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:50.470
So after about,
again, another year

00:17:50.470 --> 00:17:54.440
working with Houston, one of
our engineers came in and said,

00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:58.660
hey, I've been playing around
with this tool out of Netflix

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:00.730
called Spinnaker
to do some things,

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:03.480
and I think it would help
solve a lot of problems for us.

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:06.670
And I'm not going to get into
all the deep down nitty-gritty

00:18:06.670 --> 00:18:07.570
about Spinnaker.

00:18:07.570 --> 00:18:10.700
There's lots of
resources out there.

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:13.870
But ultimately,
fundamentally, Spinnaker

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:16.420
is an immutable
infrastructure tool, right?

00:18:16.420 --> 00:18:19.960
It's built around those
concepts of immutability.

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:22.270
And this was a very seismic
shift for our developers

00:18:22.270 --> 00:18:24.228
and our infrastructure
to start thinking about.

00:18:24.228 --> 00:18:27.640
We had to rethink many, many
parts of our platform, right?

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:30.100
We had to rethink how we were
getting runtime configuration

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:30.610
and Secrets.

00:18:30.610 --> 00:18:34.540
We had to rethink how we
were packaging applications.

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:36.430
So we needed to start
small because there

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:39.460
were going to be a lot of moving
parts, and a lot of things

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:41.290
that we needed to
change and fix.

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:44.230
So we started with a small
pilot group of dev teams

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:47.530
that we knew could
move and make changes

00:18:47.530 --> 00:18:52.914
as we found out and learned
lessons, and things like that

00:18:52.914 --> 00:18:54.830
And as we started rolling
this out and working

00:18:54.830 --> 00:18:58.310
with these pilot teams, we
learned a lot about Spinnaker,

00:18:58.310 --> 00:18:58.810
right?

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:02.300
Because no one had the
experience with it yet.

00:19:02.300 --> 00:19:04.030
But we knew that
Spinnaker's pipelines

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:07.930
were very robust at modeling how
we wanted to roll applications

00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:08.965
out to places.

00:19:08.965 --> 00:19:10.840
It gives you just about
anything you need out

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:14.730
of the box to do that.

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:17.770
But we didn't use all
of it all the time.

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:19.650
Most of our development
teams just simply

00:19:19.650 --> 00:19:22.170
need to get an
application out, right?

00:19:22.170 --> 00:19:23.682
It's a stateless application.

00:19:23.682 --> 00:19:25.390
They need to bring up
a new server group,

00:19:25.390 --> 00:19:27.056
they need to get
traffic to it, and they

00:19:27.056 --> 00:19:29.100
need to shut down
the old one, right?

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:31.410
So while Spinnaker
has lots of ability

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:34.830
to model complex workflows,
we didn't necessarily

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:39.030
need it all out of the gate.

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:41.580
Where we did need it was to
support our infrastructure

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:44.450
teams, so things that weren't
stateless applications,

00:19:44.450 --> 00:19:47.100
Elasticsearch, Kafka,
Cassandra, things

00:19:47.100 --> 00:19:49.500
like this where we needed
much more refined control

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:53.320
in how we were deploying
large clusters of things.

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:55.276
And Spinnaker allowed
us to do that.

00:19:55.276 --> 00:19:57.400
So not only did it solve
the simple problem for us,

00:19:57.400 --> 00:19:59.858
it also allowed us to solve
the difficult problems as well.

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:05.820
So we did this pilot.

00:20:08.305 --> 00:20:10.680
We were able to get better
visibility into our deployment

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:11.640
footprints.

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:15.210
We were getting
better consistency.

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:18.690
But our dev teams were
starting to tell us,

00:20:18.690 --> 00:20:22.630
you're abstracting us too far
away from the infrastructure.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:27.049
We want to be right on the
API, and things like that.

00:20:27.049 --> 00:20:28.590
And the biggest
frustration that they

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:32.010
gave us is, it's three
years and you're giving us

00:20:32.010 --> 00:20:33.510
a third platform, right?

00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:37.140
So three years in a row,
we'd asked them to change.

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:41.570
So they were getting a little
bit frustrated on that.

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:45.740
So we had to stop and ask
ourselves, OK, do we do this?

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:47.680
Do we push this across
the whole organization?

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:51.030
Was there enough
value in doing it?

00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:52.570
And unsurprisingly, yes.

00:20:52.570 --> 00:20:54.190
If the answer to
this was no, it'd

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:55.670
be a very anti-climactic talk.

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:02.930
So if we look at the cost value
of what Spinnaker brought us

00:21:02.930 --> 00:21:05.720
at the end was
this simplification

00:21:05.720 --> 00:21:07.020
of the infrastructure.

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:08.590
So the primitives
that Spinnaker uses

00:21:08.590 --> 00:21:13.460
to talk about applications, such
as server groups and clusters,

00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:17.310
model any type of application
delivery really, really well.

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:20.000
And then on top of that, it
uses a naming base pattern

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:22.310
to correlate and
aggregate infrastructure

00:21:22.310 --> 00:21:25.130
so that you get kind of an
application graph of what

00:21:25.130 --> 00:21:28.560
belongs to an application.

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:31.501
It also has a really, really
good workflow orchestration

00:21:31.501 --> 00:21:32.000
engine.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:35.090
And if you've ever implemented
a workflow engine of any kind,

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:37.690
you know that they're hard.

00:21:37.690 --> 00:21:40.250
So Spinnaker's engine,
it's very scalable.

00:21:40.250 --> 00:21:42.230
It supports resuming
in error conditions,

00:21:42.230 --> 00:21:44.600
or even restarts up it.

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:45.530
It's flexible.

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:46.490
It's fast.

00:21:46.490 --> 00:21:49.190
And companies like
Google and Netflix

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:53.210
are using it to drive
giant deployments

00:21:53.210 --> 00:21:56.090
across their organization,
so we know it's battle tested

00:21:56.090 --> 00:21:58.540
and it works well.

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:02.080
And finally, if you heard our
CIO at the keynote yesterday

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:09.660
morning, Target wants to
support open-source communities

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:10.494
as much as possible.

00:22:10.494 --> 00:22:12.326
We leverage a lot of
open-source technology,

00:22:12.326 --> 00:22:13.980
and we want to
contribute back to that.

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:16.140
So Spinnaker, as
an open-source tool

00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:20.580
with backings of many
companies, big and small,

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:23.760
was a great place for us
to get into that community.

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.352
So we could leverage their
knowledge, we could give back,

00:22:26.352 --> 00:22:28.060
we could participate
in that whole thing.

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:35.555
So we've decided to move on
to a Spinnaker at this point.

00:22:35.555 --> 00:22:37.680
And we have to figure out,
how do we move Spinnaker

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:42.840
out into the rest
of Target, right?

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:46.020
The first part is, we needed
to add support for Target's

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:49.350
private data centers.

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:51.840
So Spinnaker ships with a
number of cloud provider

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:53.760
implementations, but
not all of the ones

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:56.190
that we use in our
infrastructure were supported.

00:22:56.190 --> 00:22:58.530
So we put together
a development team,

00:22:58.530 --> 00:23:00.814
and we joined them up in
the Spinnaker community.

00:23:00.814 --> 00:23:01.980
And they were highly active.

00:23:01.980 --> 00:23:03.700
They collaborated
with some other teams,

00:23:03.700 --> 00:23:05.730
and they started
contributing back

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.310
major implementations of
Spinnaker's underlying code

00:23:08.310 --> 00:23:10.480
to support our infrastructure.

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:14.400
We also had to roll out
additional integration

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:17.100
with other platforms that
we have, such as Kubernetes

00:23:17.100 --> 00:23:18.270
and things like that.

00:23:18.270 --> 00:23:20.790
In order to facilitate
this internally,

00:23:20.790 --> 00:23:22.650
we started organizing
flash builds

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:26.027
between development teams
and our platform engineers

00:23:26.027 --> 00:23:28.110
to kind of sit with them
and be embedded with them

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:29.820
and move them onto
these platforms.

00:23:29.820 --> 00:23:33.840
Because some of them
were moving from n

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:37.350
minus two platforms
from Chef into this,

00:23:37.350 --> 00:23:39.990
and let alone from the previous
platform, from Houston.

00:23:43.540 --> 00:23:46.290
So in the end, Target's
topology looks something

00:23:46.290 --> 00:23:51.930
like this within our private
data center and public cloud.

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.210
We use OpenStack
for VM deployments.

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:56.610
We use Kubernetes
in our data center.

00:23:56.610 --> 00:23:59.460
We use Google Cloud
Platform out on the edge.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:03.160
We use other providers
on top of that as well.

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:05.610
So we're using quite a few
of the components inside

00:24:05.610 --> 00:24:08.610
of Spinnaker at any given time.

00:24:08.610 --> 00:24:11.520
And one of the things we
learned is, we probably

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:13.380
should have stopped
and rewound and thought

00:24:13.380 --> 00:24:15.421
about how we were doing
things, because you

00:24:15.421 --> 00:24:16.920
need to think about
how you're going

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:20.430
to be onboarding users and
applications into Spinnaker.

00:24:20.430 --> 00:24:22.470
Because it's based
on this static name,

00:24:22.470 --> 00:24:25.110
because it's based on
a naming convention,

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:28.020
and because you're dealing
with immutable infrastructure,

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:31.161
if you don't adhere to those
patterns right out of the gate,

00:24:31.161 --> 00:24:32.660
you get into a
position where you're

00:24:32.660 --> 00:24:34.620
having to move a lot of
things later in order

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:38.520
to make a better organization.

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:40.560
If you're looking at
running Spinnaker at scale,

00:24:40.560 --> 00:24:45.720
you also need to understand
how Spinnaker's configuration

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:49.319
works, what the different
microservice components do,

00:24:49.319 --> 00:24:51.360
and how you're going to
manage that configuration

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:53.500
and roll out changes
to your users.

00:24:53.500 --> 00:24:55.750
Because if you're giving
your developers direct access

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:58.140
onto Spinnaker, you don't
want to be bringing it down

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:02.340
for them, right, because they
can't run their deployments

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:04.085
or manage their applications.

00:25:07.532 --> 00:25:08.990
And finally, you
need to understand

00:25:08.990 --> 00:25:11.360
exactly how the different
provider implementations

00:25:11.360 --> 00:25:13.670
in Spinnaker that you're
going to be using actually

00:25:13.670 --> 00:25:16.336
interface with those
downstream providers,

00:25:16.336 --> 00:25:17.960
because this is going
to greatly impact

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:20.870
how you can scale and manage
performance of Spinnaker

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:21.957
itself.

00:25:21.957 --> 00:25:23.540
So here's where we're
going to deviate

00:25:23.540 --> 00:25:25.820
into kind of a quick expert
section of Spinnaker.

00:25:25.820 --> 00:25:27.740
So I'll ask, is
anyone here running

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:32.600
Spinnaker at their companies?

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:36.040
All right, so some.

00:25:36.040 --> 00:25:38.980
So scaling concerns as you
start bringing Spinnaker up.

00:25:38.980 --> 00:25:43.480
If you're talking about
something like Target's scale,

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:46.030
not all provider
implementations in Spinnaker

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:48.250
are built the same.

00:25:48.250 --> 00:25:51.310
So you can easily
run into problems

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:53.920
where, depending on
the implementation,

00:25:53.920 --> 00:25:56.380
you could run into things
like API rate limit problems,

00:25:56.380 --> 00:26:00.045
or even overloading the
downstream provider APIs.

00:26:00.045 --> 00:26:01.420
So you want to
look at how you're

00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:04.060
tuning some of those
polling intervals,

00:26:04.060 --> 00:26:07.690
if you can do caching of
APIs and things like that.

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:09.790
The AWS implementation
and Spinnaker

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:14.970
has something called edda
that lets you cache API calls.

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:17.640
Also, make sure you have
enough memory to run things.

00:26:17.640 --> 00:26:20.680
Because Spinnaker is collecting
data about your infrastructure,

00:26:20.680 --> 00:26:24.450
it has to collect the current
state of all your deployments

00:26:24.450 --> 00:26:28.290
before it can flush it into
its cache to do queries.

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:34.130
So you need to have that room to
get data as you're scaling out.

00:26:34.130 --> 00:26:36.470
And finally, by
default, Spinnaker

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:39.740
uses Redis as a caching agent
for most of its components.

00:26:39.740 --> 00:26:41.240
And Redis is single-threaded.

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:44.450
So at any time, only one thing
can be reading and writing

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:46.310
from Redis.

00:26:46.310 --> 00:26:48.860
What you can do, though, is
make every Spinnaker component

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:50.650
use a different Redis instance.

00:26:50.650 --> 00:26:53.360
And Redis instances are cheap.

00:26:53.360 --> 00:26:55.370
Any cloud provider
you're going to be using

00:26:55.370 --> 00:26:58.940
has managed Redis or
something compatible,

00:26:58.940 --> 00:27:00.890
and just from day
one just deploy

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:03.320
a Redis instance
since for each thing.

00:27:03.320 --> 00:27:07.390
Don't fall into the trap
of using a single Redis.

00:27:07.390 --> 00:27:09.220
It also has some more
advanced concepts

00:27:09.220 --> 00:27:13.660
where you can manage sharding
mechanisms inside of Spinnaker

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:17.900
itself by application
or origin or user

00:27:17.900 --> 00:27:22.260
so that you can scale different
parts of the componentry.

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:23.860
So Spinnaker
deployment at Target

00:27:23.860 --> 00:27:25.570
looks something like this.

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:27.340
There's a component
inside of Spinnaker

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:29.740
that's called Clouddriver
that's responsible for talking

00:27:29.740 --> 00:27:32.860
to the downstream provider APIs.

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:35.110
And we run those in
both a read/write

00:27:35.110 --> 00:27:36.610
and a read-only mode.

00:27:36.610 --> 00:27:38.950
So we have one fleet
of Clouddrivers

00:27:38.950 --> 00:27:41.830
that can make the
mutate operations

00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:42.977
into our infrastructure.

00:27:42.977 --> 00:27:44.560
And we have another
fleet that is just

00:27:44.560 --> 00:27:46.960
purely responsible for
gathering the current state

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:49.000
of that infrastructure back.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:50.560
Those Clouddriver
implementations

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:54.070
talk to fleets of
read/write Redis instances

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:56.880
that are replicating their
data to read-only instances

00:27:56.880 --> 00:27:57.380
of Redis.

00:27:57.380 --> 00:27:59.360
So we can manage that.

00:27:59.360 --> 00:28:00.970
And in between the
two, we actually

00:28:00.970 --> 00:28:03.100
run an open-source
application called

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:06.790
2N proxy that allows us to
shard data across multiple Redis

00:28:06.790 --> 00:28:07.300
instances.

00:28:07.300 --> 00:28:10.435
So we can kind of get that
feel of a multi-threaded Redis

00:28:10.435 --> 00:28:13.810
instance even though
it's single-threaded.

00:28:13.810 --> 00:28:16.360
And this whole thing is
automated and configured

00:28:16.360 --> 00:28:18.580
in such a way that
if we even need

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:21.610
to bring up a whole new
Spinnaker installation in order

00:28:21.610 --> 00:28:24.840
to support something, we
can do that at, effectively,

00:28:24.840 --> 00:28:25.980
the press of a button.

00:28:30.700 --> 00:28:33.595
So in the end, we ended up with
a typology, or an installation,

00:28:33.595 --> 00:28:35.970
of Spinnaker that has multiple
scaling dimensions, right?

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:38.280
We can scale everywhere
from the cache up

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:42.090
to the individual Spinnaker
components, and then Spinnaker

00:28:42.090 --> 00:28:43.860
installations themselves.

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:46.860
And we can adjust the right
knob for whatever performance

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:50.880
problem we're seeing, or if
we've just suddenly increased

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:55.240
load or scale on something.

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:58.222
And this ended up being a
really good process for us,

00:28:58.222 --> 00:28:59.930
because we did run
into situations where,

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:02.310
before we had this
topology, we would

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:04.730
run into some type of
scale-induced thing,

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:07.666
and we wouldn't be able
to adjust correctly.

00:29:11.900 --> 00:29:13.870
So that's where we are
in our private data

00:29:13.870 --> 00:29:16.510
center and our public
cloud usage right now.

00:29:16.510 --> 00:29:18.950
Most of our app teams are
using Spinnaker to deploy

00:29:18.950 --> 00:29:20.530
to VM-based workloads.

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:22.450
Some of them are using
Spinnaker to deploy

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:24.449
to Kubernetes-based workloads.

00:29:24.449 --> 00:29:26.740
But we are starting to see
a little bit of a divergence

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:28.732
here where a lot of app
teams just want to--

00:29:28.732 --> 00:29:30.190
if they're on
Kubernetes, they just

00:29:30.190 --> 00:29:31.570
want to talk Kubernetes, right?

00:29:31.570 --> 00:29:32.890
They want to use kubectl.

00:29:32.890 --> 00:29:34.150
They want to use Helm.

00:29:34.150 --> 00:29:36.100
They want to go straight
to that cluster,

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:39.550
and they want to use native
communities capabilities

00:29:39.550 --> 00:29:40.540
across there.

00:29:40.540 --> 00:29:46.350
So we've gotten a bit of a
divergence there happening.

00:29:46.350 --> 00:29:48.430
And as we talk about
Kubernetes, I'd

00:29:48.430 --> 00:29:50.680
be remiss if I didn't
talk about Target stores.

00:29:50.680 --> 00:29:53.600
So not only do we have private
data center and public cloud,

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:58.835
but Target has 1,800 stores
across the United States.

00:29:58.835 --> 00:30:01.210
And in each one of these stores
and distribution centers,

00:30:01.210 --> 00:30:02.530
we run a Kubernetes cluster.

00:30:05.090 --> 00:30:08.540
But because of the
distribution of these stores

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:11.990
and these distribution centers,
and the connectivity that we

00:30:11.990 --> 00:30:13.610
have with them, we
have to treat them

00:30:13.610 --> 00:30:16.375
as though they're completely
isolated cloud environments

00:30:16.375 --> 00:30:17.250
from our perspective.

00:30:17.250 --> 00:30:18.874
We can't be back
hauling data from them

00:30:18.874 --> 00:30:23.300
all the way back to Minneapolis
all the time, right?

00:30:23.300 --> 00:30:26.060
So effectively, we have
1,800 cloud providers

00:30:26.060 --> 00:30:28.280
that we have to deal
with at any given time.

00:30:33.330 --> 00:30:34.940
So how do we manage this?

00:30:34.940 --> 00:30:37.160
How do we manage
rolling software out

00:30:37.160 --> 00:30:40.400
to 1,800 disparate clouds?

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:44.210
So the first thing that we do
is we group our stores into kind

00:30:44.210 --> 00:30:47.960
of groupings and environments
based on things like geography,

00:30:47.960 --> 00:30:52.370
or size of the
store, or features,

00:30:52.370 --> 00:30:54.530
or technology that we
have installed in them,

00:30:54.530 --> 00:30:55.926
and things like that.

00:30:55.926 --> 00:30:57.800
So these environments
can consist of anywhere

00:30:57.800 --> 00:31:00.861
from one store to 300 stores.

00:31:00.861 --> 00:31:03.110
And when we push new software
loads out to the stores,

00:31:03.110 --> 00:31:04.940
we roll them out to
these environments

00:31:04.940 --> 00:31:07.550
in a sort of batch
type fashion, right?

00:31:07.550 --> 00:31:11.480
So if I push to an environment
that has 50 stores in it,

00:31:11.480 --> 00:31:14.240
all 50 stores are going to
get that same software load

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:15.360
and be running it.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:17.890
If I'm going to roll that
back, all 50 of those stores

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:21.240
roll back that software.

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:23.510
So how does Spinnaker
understand this kind

00:31:23.510 --> 00:31:25.880
of weird grouping
thing that we're

00:31:25.880 --> 00:31:30.610
doing with all these stores?

00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:33.040
You probably haven't read the
Target blog, but if you had,

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:36.600
we recently posted a thing,
and it's called Unimatrix.

00:31:36.600 --> 00:31:39.880
So Unimatrix is a tool
that we built in-house

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:42.310
to manage our store deployments.

00:31:42.310 --> 00:31:47.590
And it effectively is a
Kubernetes API facade layer.

00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:50.620
So it presents a
Kubernetes-compliant API,

00:31:50.620 --> 00:31:55.350
ReplicaSets, and Pods services,
ConfigMaps, the whole spiel,

00:31:55.350 --> 00:31:58.850
and it presents those store
groupings as name spaces

00:31:58.850 --> 00:32:00.200
onto that API.

00:32:00.200 --> 00:32:02.880
So we point our
Spinnaker instance

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:06.980
at the Unimatrix server
running in our data center,

00:32:06.980 --> 00:32:09.500
and it distributes
desired state out

00:32:09.500 --> 00:32:12.620
to each of the stores
in that namespace.

00:32:12.620 --> 00:32:15.770
And the Unimatrix agent
there then applies that state

00:32:15.770 --> 00:32:17.750
to the local Kubernetes
cluster, and is

00:32:17.750 --> 00:32:21.320
responsible for recovering
status and sending it back

00:32:21.320 --> 00:32:22.970
to us.

00:32:22.970 --> 00:32:24.650
So from Spinnaker's
point of view,

00:32:24.650 --> 00:32:32.280
we have one Kubernetes cluster
running for all 1,800 stores.

00:32:32.280 --> 00:32:35.230
And this allows us to reason
about very large scale

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:37.360
deployments in
very simple terms,

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:40.480
for both our developers and any
operations and support staff

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:41.856
that we have to deal with.

00:32:41.856 --> 00:32:44.230
It helps us eliminate deployment
drift across these store

00:32:44.230 --> 00:32:45.350
environments.

00:32:45.350 --> 00:32:49.420
And we're adhering and utilizing
open-source API specification,

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:51.880
which means any of the
tooling out there that's

00:32:51.880 --> 00:32:54.700
built against Kubernetes
API we can continue

00:32:54.700 --> 00:32:57.340
to use against our
store deployments,

00:32:57.340 --> 00:32:59.620
even though we have 1,800
of them behind the scenes.

00:33:04.360 --> 00:33:06.622
So what have we learned
from running Unimatrix?

00:33:06.622 --> 00:33:08.080
We've been running
this for a while

00:33:08.080 --> 00:33:10.680
now in our store
deployments, and we've

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:14.610
learned that abstracting the
infrastructure is good, right?

00:33:14.610 --> 00:33:16.650
It allows us to
reduce that complexity

00:33:16.650 --> 00:33:18.930
and make better decisions
about what should be done.

00:33:18.930 --> 00:33:21.810
That doesn't mean our developers
shouldn't interact directly

00:33:21.810 --> 00:33:23.010
with that API.

00:33:23.010 --> 00:33:24.540
Many of our stores'
developers still

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:28.170
use kubectl to talk to Unimatrix
in order to perform operations

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:31.350
in the stores' environments.

00:33:31.350 --> 00:33:33.405
We also needed to start
thinking about, well,

00:33:33.405 --> 00:33:37.220
what really is an application
or deployment, right?

00:33:37.220 --> 00:33:41.060
And so Unimatrix helps
us distill those concepts

00:33:41.060 --> 00:33:43.310
down to what should
be running, how is it

00:33:43.310 --> 00:33:47.210
configured, what's its current
state, things like that.

00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:50.150
And it also kind of taught us
that this microservice thing

00:33:50.150 --> 00:33:51.980
is really, really good
because we can just

00:33:51.980 --> 00:33:56.450
replace pieces of
componentry where we see fit

00:33:56.450 --> 00:33:59.978
with our opinions or patterns or
optimizations that work for us.

00:34:02.910 --> 00:34:06.300
So that's our current
state today at Target.

00:34:06.300 --> 00:34:08.670
And about six months
ago, we started

00:34:08.670 --> 00:34:13.080
working on a new project
because our current state

00:34:13.080 --> 00:34:16.650
is one where we still have many
different ways of doing things,

00:34:16.650 --> 00:34:17.190
right?

00:34:17.190 --> 00:34:19.920
We're still talking to some
Kubernetes clusters directly.

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:21.420
We're using Spinnaker in places.

00:34:21.420 --> 00:34:23.969
We're using Unimatrix for
stores, et cetera, right?

00:34:23.969 --> 00:34:26.449
And we really want to
get to this paradigm of,

00:34:26.449 --> 00:34:28.920
there should just be one
way of writing and running

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:31.679
and deploying an
application at Target.

00:34:31.679 --> 00:34:33.360
And the way that
we think about it

00:34:33.360 --> 00:34:36.261
is the idea of a
midnight developer.

00:34:36.261 --> 00:34:38.760
And Dan Woods, our distinguished
engineer, had a good point,

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:40.259
had a really good
statement of this,

00:34:40.259 --> 00:34:44.790
says a developer
at Target has to be

00:34:44.790 --> 00:34:47.219
able to build an application
in the middle of the night

00:34:47.219 --> 00:34:49.800
without having to
interact with anyone,

00:34:49.800 --> 00:34:52.620
with no previous
knowledge of our platform.

00:34:52.620 --> 00:34:55.639
If they can't do that, then
we've failed as an engineering

00:34:55.639 --> 00:34:58.270
and as a platform
organization, right?

00:34:58.270 --> 00:35:00.270
And so, that's really
kind of the guidance

00:35:00.270 --> 00:35:04.830
of what we've been trying to
do over the last six months.

00:35:04.830 --> 00:35:07.836
And so there's kind of six main
points of how we're designing

00:35:07.836 --> 00:35:08.960
and building that platform.

00:35:08.960 --> 00:35:11.770
So first, we have to have a
self-service platform, right?

00:35:11.770 --> 00:35:15.160
Our developers can't be sending
emails or issuing pull requests

00:35:15.160 --> 00:35:17.830
or doing things and waiting
for people to get back

00:35:17.830 --> 00:35:19.270
to them a day later.

00:35:19.270 --> 00:35:21.564
We have to have a common
pattern of usability, right?

00:35:21.564 --> 00:35:23.980
Regardless of where they're
running on the infrastructure,

00:35:23.980 --> 00:35:27.950
they need be able to write
that application one time.

00:35:27.950 --> 00:35:30.890
They need to be able to discover
the features of that platform.

00:35:30.890 --> 00:35:34.831
It needs to be highly available
so that they can do their job.

00:35:34.831 --> 00:35:36.580
And the way that we
can do that is through

00:35:36.580 --> 00:35:38.800
applying some strong
opinions but still

00:35:38.800 --> 00:35:41.530
allowing for flexibility
within the infrastructure

00:35:41.530 --> 00:35:43.640
and the platform itself.

00:35:43.640 --> 00:35:46.190
And finally, as
platform engineers,

00:35:46.190 --> 00:35:48.730
we need to treat everything
as an application.

00:35:48.730 --> 00:35:51.040
If we're going to be asking
our developers to follow

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:53.050
these processes or
use these opinions

00:35:53.050 --> 00:35:55.270
or run on this platform,
then we better be, too.

00:35:59.350 --> 00:36:01.621
So over the last
six months, we've

00:36:01.621 --> 00:36:03.870
been building a new platform
called Target Application

00:36:03.870 --> 00:36:05.750
Platform, or TAP.

00:36:05.750 --> 00:36:09.300
And it's combining
the best pieces

00:36:09.300 --> 00:36:10.920
of all the different
tools and all

00:36:10.920 --> 00:36:12.586
the different
infrastructures that we've

00:36:12.586 --> 00:36:14.100
dealt with over
the last five years

00:36:14.100 --> 00:36:16.680
as we've come along
on this journey.

00:36:16.680 --> 00:36:20.200
We're combining the power of
Kubernetes' declarative API

00:36:20.200 --> 00:36:22.230
along with Spinnaker's
imperative workflow

00:36:22.230 --> 00:36:24.870
engine to orchestrate
deployments

00:36:24.870 --> 00:36:26.670
so that we can manage
our infrastructure

00:36:26.670 --> 00:36:30.840
as a ubiquitous experience
for our developers, right?

00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:33.030
And so, as part of that,
we're moving our developers

00:36:33.030 --> 00:36:34.790
up the stack.

00:36:34.790 --> 00:36:36.870
We're getting them up off
of those infrastructure

00:36:36.870 --> 00:36:40.800
APIs so that our infrastructure
teams can innovate and move

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:43.260
fast and do the things that
they need to do in order

00:36:43.260 --> 00:36:45.270
to make the
infrastructure better

00:36:45.270 --> 00:36:48.840
without impacting the
application developers.

00:36:48.840 --> 00:36:52.260
I recently had a meeting with
one of our big development

00:36:52.260 --> 00:36:54.870
groups, and the director
there was explaining

00:36:54.870 --> 00:36:59.640
that his team is now accounting
in actual number of what

00:36:59.640 --> 00:37:02.860
they referred to as the
tech tax on their team.

00:37:02.860 --> 00:37:05.610
And this tech tax is
the time that they're

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:09.210
losing building business
value because infrastructure

00:37:09.210 --> 00:37:10.770
teams keep coming
and asking them

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:16.740
to migrate or do new things by
XYZ date for ABC reason, right?

00:37:16.740 --> 00:37:19.610
And that's really what we
want to get rid of, right?

00:37:19.610 --> 00:37:22.970
We need to stop doing that as
an infrastructure organization,

00:37:22.970 --> 00:37:24.960
or as platform developers.

00:37:24.960 --> 00:37:28.590
We need to allow app developers
to focus on building business

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:32.930
value and stop worrying
about the infrastructure.

00:37:32.930 --> 00:37:35.210
So we want to reduce
that cognitive load, that

00:37:35.210 --> 00:37:36.710
write once, run anywhere.

00:37:36.710 --> 00:37:38.960
I shouldn't have to change
my application in order

00:37:38.960 --> 00:37:41.570
to move it from a
container-based runtime

00:37:41.570 --> 00:37:43.715
to a VM-based runtime.

00:37:43.715 --> 00:37:45.840
I need to make sure that
my workloads are deploying

00:37:45.840 --> 00:37:48.984
consistently regardless of where
they are in the infrastructure.

00:37:48.984 --> 00:37:50.400
And I need my
infrastructure teams

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:52.680
to be able to do
their job by upgrading

00:37:52.680 --> 00:37:54.930
clusters or innovating
new technologies,

00:37:54.930 --> 00:37:57.060
and building things
that can span

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:59.857
across the entire enterprise.

00:38:04.204 --> 00:38:05.620
So in order to
support this, we've

00:38:05.620 --> 00:38:08.500
decided to take what we
learned from Unimatrix

00:38:08.500 --> 00:38:11.530
and extend it to all of
Target's infrastructure.

00:38:11.530 --> 00:38:14.530
So we settled on saying all
infrastructure at Target, when

00:38:14.530 --> 00:38:19.630
it comes to runtime deployments
or workload deployment

00:38:19.630 --> 00:38:23.020
and running, has to be managed
via the Kubernetes API.

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:25.750
So we talk about
all workloads on TAP

00:38:25.750 --> 00:38:28.420
as though they're a ReplicaSet
and a Pod, regardless

00:38:28.420 --> 00:38:31.090
of if it's on a Kubernetes
cluster or not, right?

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:34.330
So we write layers
that interface

00:38:34.330 --> 00:38:36.250
with things like
OpenStack, and then

00:38:36.250 --> 00:38:39.835
present a Kubernetes-compliant
API on top of it.

00:38:39.835 --> 00:38:41.980
This gives us a single,
unified experience

00:38:41.980 --> 00:38:44.290
across all of our
infrastructure.

00:38:44.290 --> 00:38:46.090
It also allows us
then to deploy a type

00:38:46.090 --> 00:38:49.620
of intelligent routing layer
on top of that infrastructure

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:52.180
that Spinnaker talks
to to do deployments.

00:38:52.180 --> 00:38:55.390
Now, that routing layer,
that intelligence engine,

00:38:55.390 --> 00:38:58.990
can consume data from wherever
we want it to in order

00:38:58.990 --> 00:39:00.520
to make workload
placement decisions

00:39:00.520 --> 00:39:02.060
about your application.

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:04.240
It also allows us to
do things like, if we

00:39:04.240 --> 00:39:06.880
want to upgrade a Kubernetes
cluster that requires

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:10.090
an outage, we can just bring
up a new Kubernetes cluster,

00:39:10.090 --> 00:39:11.380
disable the old one.

00:39:11.380 --> 00:39:13.480
The existing workloads
will continue running,

00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:20.159
and all new deployments will
go to the new cluster, right?

00:39:20.159 --> 00:39:21.950
So in order to make
this work, and if we're

00:39:21.950 --> 00:39:24.080
going to use the
Kubernetes API everywhere,

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:27.020
we have to model
a concept of what

00:39:27.020 --> 00:39:29.750
an application is so
that it works across all

00:39:29.750 --> 00:39:30.950
of these things, right?

00:39:30.950 --> 00:39:32.690
So we have to talk about
what we're going to deploy.

00:39:32.690 --> 00:39:34.273
And since it's
Kubernetes, we're going

00:39:34.273 --> 00:39:36.440
to be deploying
Docker containers.

00:39:36.440 --> 00:39:39.650
So application developers
at Target on TAP,

00:39:39.650 --> 00:39:42.997
during their CI process, build
and publish a Docker container.

00:39:42.997 --> 00:39:44.330
And that's the end of the story.

00:39:44.330 --> 00:39:45.746
They'll never have
to change that.

00:39:45.746 --> 00:39:48.110
They'll never have to
do anything different.

00:39:48.110 --> 00:39:50.900
They just need to tell us how
much CPU and memory they want.

00:39:50.900 --> 00:39:52.816
They need to tell us
what network port they're

00:39:52.816 --> 00:39:55.377
going to accept traffic on.

00:39:55.377 --> 00:39:57.710
They need to tell us, how do
they want their application

00:39:57.710 --> 00:39:58.870
to roll out, right?

00:39:58.870 --> 00:40:00.500
What should we do
with the old version?

00:40:00.500 --> 00:40:02.720
How many Replicas should we run?

00:40:02.720 --> 00:40:04.456
And finally, they
need to tell us,

00:40:04.456 --> 00:40:06.080
how do we know that
your application is

00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:08.240
healthy at any
given time, right?

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:12.920
Those are fundamentally the
core concepts of an application.

00:40:16.140 --> 00:40:17.707
So if everything's
a Docker container

00:40:17.707 --> 00:40:19.540
and that's what our
developers are building,

00:40:19.540 --> 00:40:21.350
how do we do things
like run on OpenStack?

00:40:21.350 --> 00:40:24.550
Well, our layer that sits
in front of OpenStack then,

00:40:24.550 --> 00:40:27.770
when it receives a deployment
command as a ReplicaSet,

00:40:27.770 --> 00:40:31.400
can call out to our enterprise
bakery, which is actually

00:40:31.400 --> 00:40:33.440
one of the subcomponents
of Spinnaker

00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:37.190
that we've just extracted
and are running standalone

00:40:37.190 --> 00:40:39.260
without the rest of Spinnaker.

00:40:39.260 --> 00:40:43.010
And so we do a just-in-time bake
of a VM image of that Docker

00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:43.820
container.

00:40:43.820 --> 00:40:46.640
So we translate that Docker
container into a VM deployment,

00:40:46.640 --> 00:40:49.190
and then tell OpenStack
to go run that.

00:40:49.190 --> 00:40:52.340
So we're able to use all of
Spinnaker's built-in abilities

00:40:52.340 --> 00:40:55.790
for modeling
container-based deployments.

00:40:55.790 --> 00:40:59.210
We can use triggering, and
we can do all the stuff

00:40:59.210 --> 00:41:00.620
that we want to do.

00:41:00.620 --> 00:41:02.660
But we can extend it
across any infrastructure

00:41:02.660 --> 00:41:04.370
that we want to bring in.

00:41:04.370 --> 00:41:07.680
And tomorrow, when one
of my developers says,

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:10.160
hey, we want to use
Nomad or something,

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:13.460
we can plug Nomad into this
system with very little effort

00:41:13.460 --> 00:41:15.750
and not impact our
application developers.

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:18.080
So we could start testing
workload deployments on there

00:41:18.080 --> 00:41:18.746
if we wanted to.

00:41:21.230 --> 00:41:23.030
We also have to provide
a consistent set

00:41:23.030 --> 00:41:26.420
of runtime primitives to
our applications, right?

00:41:26.420 --> 00:41:29.030
If we were running on Kubernetes
and our developers were using

00:41:29.030 --> 00:41:31.280
ConfigMaps and
Secrets and services

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:33.860
and all that stuff
via a cluster,

00:41:33.860 --> 00:41:36.020
they'd be locked into
the Kubernetes ecosystem,

00:41:36.020 --> 00:41:38.990
and they wouldn't be able to run
on something like an OpenStack

00:41:38.990 --> 00:41:41.100
if they needed to.

00:41:41.100 --> 00:41:42.250
So we couldn't do that.

00:41:42.250 --> 00:41:44.610
So we run all of those
primitives outside

00:41:44.610 --> 00:41:47.100
of the cluster-based workloads
with things like HashiCorp

00:41:47.100 --> 00:41:48.540
Consul and Vault.

00:41:48.540 --> 00:41:52.770
We use a global Fabio load
balancing layer for this.

00:41:52.770 --> 00:41:54.820
And all of our workloads
that are deployed,

00:41:54.820 --> 00:41:56.820
regardless of where it
is in the infrastructure,

00:41:56.820 --> 00:41:59.460
gets a routable IP
address for this sake.

00:41:59.460 --> 00:42:02.420
So we're not doing
any network overlay.

00:42:02.420 --> 00:42:05.810
We also provide primitives
for logs and metrics,

00:42:05.810 --> 00:42:09.110
and kind of API
proxying things so

00:42:09.110 --> 00:42:11.330
that we can reason
about applications

00:42:11.330 --> 00:42:12.650
in a very consistent manner.

00:42:12.650 --> 00:42:15.260
And this allows us
to do kind of SRE

00:42:15.260 --> 00:42:18.650
and support operations across
all the applications on TAP

00:42:18.650 --> 00:42:21.560
very readily without
having to know specifics

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:22.935
about that application.

00:42:25.749 --> 00:42:27.290
And finally, I
mentioned before, when

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:28.940
we started playing
with Spinnaker,

00:42:28.940 --> 00:42:31.430
we noticed that a
lot of our developers

00:42:31.430 --> 00:42:36.290
don't use all the capabilities
of a complex pipeline

00:42:36.290 --> 00:42:37.700
of any kind.

00:42:37.700 --> 00:42:39.920
And since we've distilled
this model of what

00:42:39.920 --> 00:42:42.410
an application or
a deployment is

00:42:42.410 --> 00:42:44.840
down to these kind
of core concepts,

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:47.840
we can also encode
a best practice

00:42:47.840 --> 00:42:49.880
of how we're going to
roll out an application

00:42:49.880 --> 00:42:51.740
into our infrastructure.

00:42:51.740 --> 00:42:54.020
And we can do that
synthetically in a way

00:42:54.020 --> 00:42:56.810
that Spinnaker doesn't
need to know the fully

00:42:56.810 --> 00:42:58.130
materialized pipeline.

00:42:58.130 --> 00:43:00.170
We can generate
it for them based

00:43:00.170 --> 00:43:03.380
on what they're telling
us about the application.

00:43:03.380 --> 00:43:05.510
So we've written
a new application

00:43:05.510 --> 00:43:08.690
that exists as part of our
Spinnaker installation.

00:43:08.690 --> 00:43:10.670
Spinnaker has a
component called Front50.

00:43:10.670 --> 00:43:13.910
It's responsible for storing
your pipeline metadata.

00:43:13.910 --> 00:43:17.220
So we wrote an
application called Back40,

00:43:17.220 --> 00:43:19.790
and it synthetically
generates pipeline information

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:22.160
based on application metadata.

00:43:22.160 --> 00:43:23.810
It also allows us
to, if we need to,

00:43:23.810 --> 00:43:26.750
inject in a fully materialized
pipeline for Spinnaker.

00:43:26.750 --> 00:43:29.150
So if we need to build some
type of complex workflow,

00:43:29.150 --> 00:43:31.351
we can do that, right?

00:43:31.351 --> 00:43:33.390
And this allows us
to kind of manage

00:43:33.390 --> 00:43:38.064
best practices for deployments
across the whole enterprise

00:43:38.064 --> 00:43:39.480
without having to
go to developers

00:43:39.480 --> 00:43:42.390
and ask them to, hey, go
modify these pipelines.

00:43:47.550 --> 00:43:50.880
So that is kind of the
Target of the future of where

00:43:50.880 --> 00:43:52.470
we're going, right?

00:43:52.470 --> 00:43:55.260
And it's been a long
journey to get there, right,

00:43:55.260 --> 00:43:56.405
long in a sense.

00:43:56.405 --> 00:43:57.780
But in the last
five years, we've

00:43:57.780 --> 00:44:00.530
tried a lot of
different things, right?

00:44:00.530 --> 00:44:03.360
We've experimented with a
lot of tools and patterns.

00:44:03.360 --> 00:44:06.350
We've learned a lot about
what our developers want to do

00:44:06.350 --> 00:44:09.150
and what they really need to do.

00:44:09.150 --> 00:44:13.830
And we've fine-tuned we treat as
an application or a deployment

00:44:13.830 --> 00:44:16.380
or workload at Target
so that we can reason

00:44:16.380 --> 00:44:17.790
about the scale a lot easier.

00:44:20.990 --> 00:44:25.160
So before I go, just my final
thoughts on all of this.

00:44:25.160 --> 00:44:27.470
If you're not doing
immutable infrastructure yet,

00:44:27.470 --> 00:44:29.270
you should probably start.

00:44:29.270 --> 00:44:31.610
It gives you a real
solid foundation

00:44:31.610 --> 00:44:34.340
for building consistent
deployment and operations

00:44:34.340 --> 00:44:36.260
patterns for your organization.

00:44:36.260 --> 00:44:40.430
And Spinnaker really will help
you drive that type of adoption

00:44:40.430 --> 00:44:41.810
if you aren't doing it yet.

00:44:41.810 --> 00:44:43.860
And it provides many
of the best practices

00:44:43.860 --> 00:44:45.680
so you don't need to
create them yourself.

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:50.460
If you're building a
platform for yourself,

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:52.590
or if you're looking to
customize these tools,

00:44:52.590 --> 00:44:55.520
don't be afraid to shape your
usage with flexible opinions.

00:44:55.520 --> 00:44:58.440
Your developers are always
going to ask for all the things.

00:44:58.440 --> 00:45:01.050
But managing scale
is a lot easier

00:45:01.050 --> 00:45:02.550
when you have
opinionated patterns

00:45:02.550 --> 00:45:05.880
of how your developers can
use those tools, right?

00:45:05.880 --> 00:45:08.250
And Spinnaker will help you
model a lot of those cloud

00:45:08.250 --> 00:45:10.720
providers in a consistent
set of primitives

00:45:10.720 --> 00:45:12.720
and how we're going to
talk about an application

00:45:12.720 --> 00:45:16.550
or how we're going to
talk about a deployment.

00:45:16.550 --> 00:45:18.680
And finally, don't be
afraid to customize, right?

00:45:18.680 --> 00:45:20.600
This is really the
power of why we

00:45:20.600 --> 00:45:25.160
went from big,
conjoined applications

00:45:25.160 --> 00:45:29.330
into microservices, right, that
are responsible for singular

00:45:29.330 --> 00:45:30.740
things, right?

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:33.680
Using open-source tooling gives
you a lot of productivity,

00:45:33.680 --> 00:45:36.290
but don't be afraid to
customize that tooling

00:45:36.290 --> 00:45:39.560
for your specific needs, if
it makes sense to, right?

00:45:39.560 --> 00:45:43.010
And in this case, Spinnaker's
design and architecture

00:45:43.010 --> 00:45:45.265
will allow you to give you
a default installation out

00:45:45.265 --> 00:45:46.640
of the box with
all the goodness.

00:45:46.640 --> 00:45:50.840
But then you can piecemeal
things if you need to,

00:45:50.840 --> 00:45:52.970
or take them and run them
completely on their own

00:45:52.970 --> 00:45:54.543
in a standalone environment.

00:45:57.790 --> 00:46:00.280
So I hope what I was
talking about today was

00:46:00.280 --> 00:46:01.430
interesting to you.

00:46:01.430 --> 00:46:02.970
I hope you have a great
rest of the conference,

00:46:02.970 --> 00:46:04.000
and thank you very much.

00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:07.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

